{"anti_rollback_limitation":"Deleting the newest event and rewinding the committed head to a still-valid earlier head is a rollback no purely local record can detect.  DurableRunIndex.head_anchor() is the exact value an external anti-rollback anchor would pin; Milestone 2 implements no such anchor.","branch":"paired-runner/m2-causal-index-and-ipc-repairs","corruption_fixture_count":24,"corruption_fixture_object_classes":["proposal","decision","reservation","lifecycle-started","lifecycle-terminal","effect-receipt","filesystem-before","filesystem-after","git-before","git-after","effect-ledger-entry","reconciliation"],"corruption_modes":["committed_byte_flip","single_canonical_field_mutation"],"fault_model":"Deterministic in-process fault injection at named points. InjectedFault derives from BaseException and deliberately skips every cleanup step, so the durable state is frozen exactly as process death would leave it. This is not a power-loss or device-level fault model; see implementation/M2_PLATFORM_AND_DURABILITY_CONTRACT.md section 5.","fault_point_count":25,"independent_oracle":"tests/test_admissible_paired_runner_m2_fault_oracle.py judges every fault point without consulting this table or any classification the implementation computes. It asserts four properties from durable bytes and the workspace itself: no effect executes twice; a verified final reconciliation implies both a durable ledger entry and an actually-performed mutation; an incomplete object set never reconciles; and no ledger entry ever asserts its own successful reconciliation.","injector_default":"inert; only a test may arm a fault point","recovery_rule":"A fresh controller reconstructs state from durable bytes only. If a STARTED record is durable the effect may have occurred, so the substrate raises AmbiguousEffectRefused and never replays. replay_permitted is false in every classification.","repair_note":"Six fault points were added by the first Milestone 2 critical repairs: an observer failure 